  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSlow moving H5 upper low continues to move to the east and southeast \nacross southern Manitoba this afternoon. Scattered showers and \nisolated thunderstorms will be possible along the International \nBorder and areas south to about Highway 2. A few stronger storms \ncould form this afternoon in northwest Minnesota. Heading into \nWednesday, showers and storms end during the early morning, with dry \nweather the remainder of the day. Temperatures will be very similar \neach day through the weekend, with widespread 70s to low 80s for \nhigh temps each afternoon. Our next chance for precipitation will be \nlate Friday through the weekend as another H5 shortwave moves slowly \nacross the Northern Plains.\n\n...Isolated Severe Thunderstorms This Evening...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are moving across northwest Minnesota this \nafternoon, most being very light. This is in response to an upper \nlow and H5 trough extending into northwest Minnesota, in the \npresence of modest instability and peak daytime heating. At \nthis time, the best forcing remains east of the area; however, \nthere is just enough forcing, along small lobes of higher \nvorticity that are moving along the flow to the south of the \nmain H5 low, to allow a mention of isolated strong to severe \nstorms this afternoon. Best chances will extend southward along \nand east of a line from Lake of the Woods to about Wadena, MN. \nThe primary concern will be hail up to 1 inch in diameter, with \na lower chance for damaging wind gusts up to 60 mph.",
